The program is small (one [38;5;10mstrcspn[0m call), so the work was in the C-isms rather than the logic:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mfgets[0m emulated byte-at-a-time: reads at most 99 bytes, keeps the newline, NUL-terminates, and on EOF-before-any-byte returns "NULL" leaving the zero-initialized buffer untouched. This is why totally-empty stdin still prints [38;5;10m0[0m.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10ms[strlen(s)-1] = '\0'[0m strips the last byte unconditionally, not just newlines — so a final line without a newline loses a real character ([38;5;10mabcde[0m alone → [38;5;10mabcd[0m), and a 99-char line loses a real character too. That behavior is preserved, not fixed.[0m[0m
- The empty-line case makes the C write [38;5;10ms[-1][0m, out of bounds. Since both arrays are zero-initialized and [38;5;10mfgets(…, 100)[0m never stores past index 99, the byte preceding either array already holds zero, so the stray store cannot be observed. I skip it in Rust rather than emulating a specific stack layout, and noted that reasoning in a comment.[0m[0m
- Buffers are [38;5;10m[u8; 100][0m with explicit [38;5;10mstrlen[0m/[38;5;10mstrcspn[0m over bytes, so embedded NULs and non-UTF-8 input behave like C (verified with [38;5;10m\x00[0m and [38;5;10m\xff[0m inputs).[0m[0m
